= Chronotype =

== Overview ==

{{:Chronotype/Simple}}

'''Chronotype''' refers to inter-individual differences in the phase of daily sleep–wake timing and preferred activity relative to the external 24-hour day. It is partly informed by endogenous circadian regulation and the light–dark cycle, but reported sleep schedules also reflect homeostatic sleep pressure and social constraints. A questionnaire classification is not a direct measurement of every circadian physiological phase marker.

== Core measurement families ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Indicator !! What is observed !! Main caveat
|-
| Morningness–Eveningness Questionnaire || Preferences for activity and timing in hypothetical situations || Self-report scale may vary with age, population and culture
|-
| Munich ChronoType Questionnaire (MCTQ) || Typical sleep times across work and free days || Work schedules, sleep debt and compensation affect observed midpoints
|-
| Sleep diary/actigraphy || Behavior over measured periods || Cannot independently identify internal clock phase
|-
| Circadian phase markers || Physiological timing, such as light-controlled melatonin onset under suitable protocols || Require appropriate laboratory/environmental control and phase interpretation
|}

Horne and Östberg's 1976 work linked a morningness–eveningness instrument to physiological temperature timing in an original sample. Roenneberg and colleagues (2003) developed distinctions between free-day and workday sleep to study human temporal patterns.<ref name="horne_core">J. A. Horne and O. Östberg, [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1027738/ “A Self-Assessment Questionnaire to Determine Morningness-Eveningness in Human Circadian Rhythms”] (1976).</ref><ref name="roen_core">T. Roenneberg, A. Wirz-Justice and M. Merrow, [https://doi.org/10.1177/0748730402239679 “Life Between Clocks: Daily Temporal Patterns of Human Chronotypes”], ''Journal of Biological Rhythms'' 18, 80–90 (2003).</ref>

== Chronotype is not one universal causal type ==

A late sleep midpoint can reflect a late internal phase, job requirements, delayed sleep due to screen/light behavior or recovery from sleep deprivation. Observed sleep and an internal circadian oscillator are coupled but distinct. No one scale fixes an immutable “lark” or “owl” biological identity for every environment.

Chronotype research relates to [[Related to::Cognition|cognitive performance]], [[Related to::Scientific model|circadian modeling]] and [[Related to::Uncertainty|measurement uncertainty]]. The relation is conceptual, not a claim that a chronotype value predicts a person's general intelligence or personality.

